WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.122 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Imagine sharing eleven quarter-sized pieces
among a group.

00:00:04.242 --> 00:00:08.227 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Counting pieces is easy, but how many whole
units could you build?

00:00:08.347 --> 00:00:11.235 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
There's more than one way to name this amount!

00:00:11.967 --> 00:00:19.420 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Today, we're learning to represent and convert
mixed numbers and improper fractions, without
changing the amount they describe.

00:00:20.033 --> 00:00:24.379 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
You'll group equal pieces to see complete wholes
and any extra part.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:29.170 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
You'll turn an improper fraction into a mixed
number, and go back the other way.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:34.049 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
You'll check that both names match the same
model or number-line position.

00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:35.828 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
First, choose one whole.

00:00:35.948 --> 00:00:38.666 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Its parts must be equal in size.

00:00:38.786 --> 00:00:43.423 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The outline of the model shows the entire whole,
not just the coloured share.

00:00:43.933 --> 00:00:47.147 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Here, three equal parts are chosen out of four.

00:00:47.267 --> 00:00:50.862 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The top number, the numerator, counts chosen
parts.

00:00:50.982 --> 00:00:55.601 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The bottom number, the denominator, tells how
many equal parts make the whole.

00:00:56.233 --> 00:00:59.860 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
How many complete wholes can you build from
eleven quarters?

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:03.935 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Group four pieces at a time, and notice any
pieces left over.

00:01:06.967 --> 00:01:08.978 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Four quarters make one whole.

00:01:09.098 --> 00:01:11.846 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Another four quarters make a second whole.

00:01:11.966 --> 00:01:14.080 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Three quarters are left over.

00:01:14.433 --> 00:01:17.558 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Eleven quarters is an improper fraction.

00:01:17.678 --> 00:01:21.486 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Its numerator is at least as large as its
denominator.

00:01:21.606 --> 00:01:24.168 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
It can describe one whole or more.

00:01:24.533 --> 00:01:27.312 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Two and three quarters is a mixed number.

00:01:27.432 --> 00:01:30.830 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
It combines two whole units with a fractional
part.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.944 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
To convert, divide eleven by four.

00:01:34.064 --> 00:01:37.996 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Eleven divided by four is two with a remainder
of three.

00:01:38.367 --> 00:01:42.431 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The denominator stays four because our pieces
are still quarters.

00:01:42.551 --> 00:01:45.892 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
So eleven quarters equals two and three
quarters.

00:01:46.267 --> 00:01:49.787 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The remainder counts extra quarter-sized pieces.

00:01:49.907 --> 00:01:52.346 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
It doesn't become the denominator.

00:01:52.466 --> 00:01:57.086 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
A picture helps you see two complete wholes and
three quarters of the next one.

00:01:57.767 --> 00:02:02.288 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
How could you count all the fifth-sized pieces
in three and two fifths?

00:02:02.408 --> 00:02:05.592 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Remember that each whole contains five fifths.

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:10.382 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Now go the other way.

00:02:10.502 --> 00:02:12.543 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Take three and two fifths.

00:02:12.663 --> 00:02:14.995 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Each whole contains five fifths.

00:02:15.367 --> 00:02:18.087 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Three times five gives fifteen fifths.

00:02:18.207 --> 00:02:21.724 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Add the extra two fifths to make seventeen
fifths.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:25.732 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
So three and two fifths equals seventeen fifths.

00:02:25.852 --> 00:02:31.256 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Multiply the whole number by the denominator,
add the numerator, and keep the denominator.

00:02:31.633 --> 00:02:35.494 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Counting in fifths doesn't change the whole or
the amount.

00:02:35.614 --> 00:02:40.780 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
It simply replaces the whole-number groups with
a single count of equal pieces.

00:02:41.433 --> 00:02:46.013 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
A mixed number isn't made by adding its whole
number to its numerator.

00:02:46.133 --> 00:02:49.344 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Two and three quarters is not five quarters.

00:02:49.464 --> 00:02:52.696 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The two wholes already contain eight quarters.

00:02:53.367 --> 00:02:56.789 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Eight quarters plus three quarters is eleven
quarters.

00:02:56.909 --> 00:03:02.591 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
That's why we multiply the whole number by the
denominator, then add the extra numerator.

00:03:03.267 --> 00:03:06.541 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
A fraction can also equal an exact whole.

00:03:06.661 --> 00:03:10.781 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Eight quarters is two wholes, with no fractional
part left over.

00:03:11.133 --> 00:03:14.550 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
To compare mixed numbers, compare the wholes
first.

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:18.369 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Three and one quarter is greater than two and
three quarters.

00:03:18.733 --> 00:03:21.976 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
If the wholes match, compare the fractional
parts.

00:03:22.096 --> 00:03:25.456 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Two and one half is greater than two and one
quarter.

00:03:25.833 --> 00:03:29.169 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
A number line helps us check amounts beyond one.

00:03:29.289 --> 00:03:32.833 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Two and one half is ten quarter-steps from zero.

00:03:32.953 --> 00:03:36.761 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Two and three quarters is one quarter-step
further to the right.

00:03:37.433 --> 00:03:40.721 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
How many wholes and quarters are in nine
quarters?

00:03:45.733 --> 00:03:48.708 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Nine quarters is two wholes and one quarter.

00:03:49.067 --> 00:03:53.020 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Our goal was to connect mixed numbers and
improper fractions.

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:57.930 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
You can build wholes, count the leftover parts,
and convert in either direction.

00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:01.237 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Before calculating, what would your amount look
like?

00:04:01.357 --> 00:04:04.638 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Try drawing it, then practise on Math Magic.
